15:57:04  INFO  [msg ← Uni] KP response from Uni (final diagnosis on acm.org / 10.0.0.99).

Root cause: this is a DNS problem, not a routing problem. 10.0.0.99 is in the RFC1918 private address range (10.0.0.0/8) and is not globally routable on the public Internet — no upstream carries it, which is correct behavior. So the IP your resolver returned for acm.org is bogus.

Evidence gathered:
1. Local audit on Uni: my route for 10.0.0.99 is just the default to AS1; no loop on my side. I reproduced the 100% loss from my own loopback (128.173.0.1).
2. Upstream WHY to AS1 (KP CANNOT): AS1 has no route to 10.0.0.99; it forwards to peer AS2, which bounces it back via ICMP Redirect — that's the loop you observed as "TTL exceeded from 10.0.1.2". AS1 also reports that AS2's customer cone advertises ACM as 198.82.0.0/24, i.e. acm.org's real address space is public, not 10.x.
3. I queried my own DNS forwarder: it also returns acm.org → 10.0.0.99. So the bad answer is being injected by the DNS path I forward to (upstream resolver), not by your laptop.

FIX status: CANNOT (pending admin action).
- The fix is on the DNS side (correct the bogus acm.org record at the upstream resolver, or reconfigure my forwarder to use a trustworthy resolver). Changing the campus DNS resolver configuration affects all users and is a security-relevant change, so it requires administrator approval — I will not apply it unilaterally. Admins have been notified.
- Workaround you can try right now: query a public resolver directly, e.g. `dig @1.1.1.1 acm.org` or `dig @8.8.8.8 acm.org`, and use that address. The real ACM web service lives in 198.82.0.0/24 per AS1.

The routing loop symptom is a downstream effect of the wrong IP; once DNS returns the correct address, connectivity should work normally over the AS1 → AS2 path.
