The network's diagnostic system has already identified the cause and notified the administrators to fix the DNS server. You don't need to contact anyone — it's in their queue. Once they correct the DNS entry, acm.org will load normally. In the meantime, other sites should work fine."

Technical summary of investigation:
- Reproduced: acm.org resolved to 10.0.0.99 (RFC1918), ping returned ICMP TTL-exceeded from 10.0.1.2, curl failed with "no route to host".
- Reported objective findings to KP via Uni.
- KP diagnosis: bogus DNS record injected by upstream resolver; 10.0.0.99 is not globally routable; real ACM space is 198.82.0.0/24. Routing loop is a downstream symptom, not the root cause.
- FIX status: CANNOT applied automatically (DNS resolver change is security-relevant and requires admin approval); admins have been notified by the KP.
